node_modules
**/node_modules
dist
**/dist
build
coverage
.git
.github
.cache
.vite
*.log
*.tsbuildinfo
# Secrets: exclude env files at EVERY depth, not only the context root.
# A bare `.env` pattern matches the root file only — `apps/api/.env`, which
# README tells developers to create and which holds JWT_SECRET, DATABASE_URL,
# OPENAI_API_KEY and CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY — was still entering the build
# context and being baked into the API image by `COPY apps/api apps/api`.
# The `**/` variants are what actually exclude it, mirroring the
# `**/node_modules` and `**/dist` entries above.
# Guarded by tests/repo-invariants.test.ts. See docs/security/encryption-at-rest.md.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
# `*.env` (e.g. apps/api/pilot.env) is gitignored (.gitignore:23) but was NOT
# dockerignored — so git would never surface it in review while Docker baked it
# into a layer. Same hole as above through a filename the patterns above miss.
*.env
**/*.env
# Key and certificate material. Nothing in the tree ships these yet, but §2.1 of
# the encryption doc anticipates per-adapter certs, and neither .gitignore nor
# .dockerignore covered them — better present before the first one appears.
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.crt
**/*.p12
# An .npmrc under an app directory is copied by the same COPY and may hold a
# registry auth token.
**/.npmrc
# Committed *.example files hold placeholders only and are safe to ship.
# These negations MUST stay last: Docker is last-match-wins, so a pattern added
# below them could silently re-admit a real secret.
!.env.example
!.env.*.example
!**/.env.example
!**/.env.*.example
